00:48:58,859 --> 00:49:03,359
As we walked across the moonlight gravel to the porch he disposed of the

628
00:49:03,359 --> 00:49:06,600
situation in a few brisk phrases.

629
00:49:06,600 --> 00:49:10,079
“I’ll telephone for a taxi to take you home, and while you’re waiting you and

630
00:49:10,079 --> 00:49:14,939
Jordan better go in the kitchen and have them get you some supper— —if you want any.”

631
00:49:14,939 --> 00:49:19,100
He opened the door. “Come in.”

632
00:49:20,279 --> 00:49:22,559
“No, thanks.

633
00:49:22,559 --> 00:49:25,439
But I’d be glad if you order me the taxi.

634
00:49:25,439 --> 00:49:30,898
I’ll wait outside.” Jordan put her hand on my arm.

635
00:49:30,898 --> 00:49:38,279
“Won’t you come in, Nick?” “No, thanks.” I was feeling a little sick and I wanted

636
00:49:38,279 --> 00:49:39,840
to be alone.

637
00:49:39,840 --> 00:49:42,719
But Jordan lingered for a moment more.

638
00:49:42,719 --> 00:49:46,079
“It’s only half past nine,” she said.

639
00:49:46,079 --> 00:49:48,238
I’d be damned if I’d go in;

640
00:49:48,238 --> 00:49:54,359
I’d had enough of all of them for one day, and suddenly that included Jordan too.

641
00:49:54,359 --> 00:49:58,379
She must have seen something of this in my expression, for she turned abruptly

642
00:49:58,379 --> 00:50:01,978
away and ran up the porch steps into the house.

643
00:50:01,978 --> 00:50:05,639
I sat down for a few minutes with my head in my hands, until I heard the phone

644
00:50:05,639 --> 00:50:09,540
taken up inside and the butler’s voice calling a taxi.

645
00:50:09,540 --> 00:50:14,520
Then I walked slowly down the drive away from the house, intending to wait by the gate.

646
00:50:14,520 --> 00:50:21,600
I hadn’t gone 20 yards when I heard my name and Gatsby stepped from between two

647
00:50:21,600 --> 00:50:23,879
bushes into the path.

648
00:50:23,879 --> 00:50:26,939
I must have felt pretty weird by that time, because I could think of nothing

649
00:50:26,939 --> 00:50:30,840
except the luminosity of his pink suit under the moon.

650
00:50:30,840 --> 00:50:33,779
“What are you doing?” I inquired.

651
00:50:33,779 --> 00:50:38,219
“Just standing here, old sport.” Somehow, that seemed a despicable

652
00:50:38,219 --> 00:50:43,260
occupation. For all I knew he was going to rob the house in a moment; I wouldn’t

653
00:50:43,260 --> 00:50:47,579
have been surprised to see sinister faces, the faces of wolfsheim's people,”

654
00:50:47,579 --> 00:50:50,398
behind him in the dark shrubbery.

655
00:50:50,398 --> 00:50:55,559
“Did you see any trouble on the road?” he asked after a minute. “Yes.”

656
00:50:55,559 --> 00:51:03,840
He hesitated. “Was she killed?” “Yes.” “I thought so; I told Daisy I thought so.

657
00:51:03,840 --> 00:51:07,079
It’s better that the shock should all come at once.

658
00:51:07,079 --> 00:51:13,439
She stood it pretty well.” He spoke as if Daisy’s reaction was the only thing that mattered.

659
00:51:13,439 --> 00:51:15,600
“I got to West dag by a side road,” he went

660
00:51:15,600 --> 00:51:18,299
on, “and left the car in my garage. I don’t

661
00:51:18,299 --> 00:51:21,238
think anybody saw us, but of course I can’t be sure.”

662
00:51:21,238 --> 00:51:25,200
I disliked him so much by this time I didn’t find it necessary to tell him he

663
00:51:25,200 --> 00:51:26,520
was wrong.

664
00:51:26,520 --> 00:51:32,100
“Who was the woman?” he inquired. “Her name was Wilson. Her husband owns the garage.

665
00:51:32,100 --> 00:51:36,180
How the devil did it happen?” “Well, I tried to swing the wheel—”

666
00:51:36,180 --> 00:51:40,079
He broke off, and suddenly I guessed it the truth.

667
00:51:40,079 --> 00:51:48,659
“Was Daisy driving?” “Yes,” he said after a moment, “but of course I’ll say I was.

668
00:51:48,659 --> 00:51:51,659
You see, when we left New York she was very nervous and she thought it would

669
00:51:51,659 --> 00:51:55,559
steady her to drive— —and this woman rushed out at us just as we were passing

670
00:51:55,559 --> 00:51:57,478
a car coming the other way.

671
00:51:57,478 --> 00:52:01,859
It all happened in a minute, but it seemed to me that she wanted to speak to

672
00:52:01,859 --> 00:52:04,500
us, thought we were somebody she knew.

673
00:52:04,500 --> 00:52:08,700
Well, first Daisy turned away from the woman toward the other car, and then she

674
00:52:08,700 --> 00:52:12,659
lost her nerve and turned back. The second my hand reached the wheel I felt

675
00:52:12,659 --> 00:52:17,579
the shock— —it must have killed her instantly.” “It ripped her open—”

676
00:52:17,579 --> 00:52:24,299
“Don’t tell me, old sport.” He winced. “Anyhow— —Daisy stepped on it. I tried to make her

677
00:52:24,299 --> 00:52:28,318
stop, but she couldn’t, so I pulled on the emergency brake. Then she fell over into

678
00:52:28,318 --> 00:52:30,420
my lap and I drove on.

679
00:52:30,420 --> 00:52:33,898
“She’ll be all right tomorrow,” he said presently.

680
00:52:33,898 --> 00:52:37,079
“I’m just going to wait here and see if he tries to bother her about that

681
00:52:37,079 --> 00:52:39,120
unpleasantness this afternoon.

682
00:52:39,120 --> 00:52:42,540
She’s locked herself into her room, and if he tries any brutality she’s going to

683
00:52:42,540 --> 00:52:48,059
turn the light out and on again.” “He won’t touch her,” I said.

684
00:52:48,059 --> 00:52:52,738
“He’s not thinking about her.” “I don’t trust him, old sport.”

685
00:52:52,738 --> 00:52:59,040
“How long are you going to wait?” “All night, if necessary. Anyhow until they all go to bed.”

686
00:52:59,040 --> 00:53:01,500
A new point of view occurred to me.

687
00:53:01,500 --> 00:53:06,000
Suppose Tom found out that Daisy had been driving.

688
00:53:06,000 --> 00:53:10,920
He might think he saw a connection in it— —he might think anything.

689
00:53:10,920 --> 00:53:15,478
I looked at the house; there were two or three bright windows downstairs and the

690
00:53:15,478 --> 00:53:19,439
pink glow from Daisy’s room on the second floor.

691
00:53:19,439 --> 00:53:21,840
“You wait here,” I said.

692
00:53:21,840 --> 00:53:25,020
“I’ll see if there’s any sign of a commotion.”

693
00:53:25,020 --> 00:53:30,299
I walked back along the border of the lawn, traversed the gravel softly, and

694
00:53:30,299 --> 00:53:33,059
tiptoed up the veranda steps.

695
00:53:33,059 --> 00:53:38,100
The drawing room curtains were open, and I saw that the room was empty.

696
00:53:38,100 --> 00:53:42,478
Crossing the porch where we had dined that June night three months before, I

697
00:53:42,478 --> 00:53:47,939
came to a small rectangle of light which I guessed was the pantry window.

698
00:53:47,939 --> 00:53:52,978
The blind was drawn, but I found a rift at the sill.

699
00:53:52,978 --> 00:53:56,398
Daisy and Tom were sitting opposite each other at the kitchen table, with a plate

700
00:53:56,398 --> 00:54:01,020
of cold fried chicken between them, and two bottles of ale.

701
00:54:01,020 --> 00:54:06,238
He was talking intently across the table at her, and in his earnestness his hands

702
00:54:06,238 --> 00:54:09,420
had fallen upon and covered her own.

703
00:54:09,420 --> 00:54:13,859
Once in a while she looked up at him and nodded in agreement.

704
00:54:13,859 --> 00:54:18,659
They weren’t happy, and neither of them had touched the chicken or the ale—

705
00:54:18,659 --> 00:54:24,120
—and yet they weren’t unhappy either was an unmistakable air of natural

706
00:54:24,120 --> 00:54:31,318
intimacy about the picture, and anybody would have said they were conspiring together.

707
00:54:31,318 --> 00:54:35,340
As I tiptoed from the porch I heard my taxi feeling its way along the dark road

708
00:54:35,340 --> 00:54:37,260
toward the house.

709
00:54:37,260 --> 00:54:41,398
Gatsby was waiting where I had left him in the drive.

710
00:54:41,398 --> 00:54:45,359
“Is it all quiet up there?” he asked anxiously.

711
00:54:45,359 --> 00:54:49,260
“Yes, it’s all quiet.” I hesitated.

712
00:54:49,260 --> 00:54:54,059
“You’d better come home and get some sleep.” He shook his head.

713
00:54:54,059 --> 00:54:57,180
“I want to wait here till Daisy goes to bed.

714
00:54:57,180 --> 00:55:01,680
Good night, old sport.” He put his hands in his coat pockets and

715
00:55:01,680 --> 00:55:07,079
turned back eagerly to his scrutiny of the house, as though my presence marred the

716
00:55:07,079 --> 00:55:09,959
sacredness of the vigil.

717
00:55:09,959 --> 00:55:17,000
So I walked away and left him standing there in the moonlight— —watching over nothing
